  "enhanced_information": "The 15-year-old girl presents with symptoms suggestive of a vascular issue, specifically vasculitis, indicated by the irregularity and stenosis of the right subclavian artery seen on MRI. The elevated blood pressure on the left arm and lower blood pressure on the right arm, along with a weaker pulse on the right side, further support this diagnosis. Prednisone was initiated and led to symptom improvement, which is consistent with the treatment of vasculitis. The next step in management should consider the severity of the condition and the response to initial therapy. Cyclophosphamide is indicated for severe cases of vasculitis, especially if there is significant vascular involvement or if the patient does not respond adequately to corticosteroids alone. While rituximab is an option for refractory cases, it is typically considered after cyclophosphamide in severe cases. Surgical intervention is generally reserved for specific complications or persistent symptoms despite medical management. Plasmapheresis is less common for this condition and is not typically the first-line treatment. Therefore, the most appropriate next step in management is to consider cyclophosphamide for further treatment of the vasculitis."
